# What does not go into the .vsix

The extension is five files' worth of shipping: `package.json`,
`src/extension.js`, `src/host.js`, `src/ws.js` and `media/**`. Everything below
is either a development-time file or something the editor does not need.

There is no `node_modules/` to exclude and there never will be one: the
manifest declares no `dependencies` and no `devDependencies`, which is the whole
reason `src/ws.js` exists. The line is here anyway, because the day somebody
runs `npm install` for a convenience nobody needed, this is the file that keeps
their node_modules out of a signed artifact.

```
# Development-time only. The tests run in a checkout, not in the editor.
test/**
**/*.test.js
node_modules/**
**/package-lock.json
**/yarn.lock
**/pnpm-lock.yaml
scripts/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/**
.vscode/**
**/.vscode/**
**/tsconfig*.json
**/.eslintrc*
**/.editorconfig

# Editor and OS noise.
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
**/*.swp
**/*~

# Never ship a built artifact inside a built artifact.
*.vsix
*.tgz

# Sources that end up in a sourcemap anyway, and are the wrong version if they
# do not.
**/*.map
```

## Deliberately kept

- `src/host.js`, `src/ws.js` and `src/extension.js`. These are the extension.
- `media/**`. This is the webview, and without it the panel has nothing to
  render.
- `PROTOCOL.md`. The contract between the webview and the bridge, in three
  hundred lines, for the benefit of whoever changes one of the three and wants
  to know what they are not allowed to break.
- `README.md` and `CHANGELOG.md`, which the marketplace shows.